{{#hasAnyOAuth2}}
### OAuth2 token lifecycle

#### Async authentication

OAuth2 authenticators resolve the access token by making an HTTP call to the token endpoint, which happens before the request carrying it is sent.

#### Refresh tokens

When an OAuth2 grant (Authorization Code, Password, or OpenID Connect) returns a `refresh_token` alongside the access token, the generated `OAuth2TokenManager` will automatically use `grant_type=refresh_token` to obtain a fresh access token when the cached one expires. If the refresh attempt fails (for example because the refresh token itself has been revoked or has expired), the token manager falls back to re-running the original grant. Client Credentials never receives a refresh token; that flow always re-runs the client-credentials grant.

#### A 401 replays the request once

When an OAuth2 or OpenID Connect call is answered with 401, the SDK drops the cached access token (the refresh token is kept; `invalidate_access_token/1` on the authenticator does the same by hand) and sends the request once more with fresh headers. A second 401 is reported as the usual `UnauthorizedError`. API key, basic and bearer authenticators never replay.

#### PKCE

The Authorization Code and OpenID Connect flows use PKCE with S256 (RFC 7636). `build_authorization_url/2` draws a new 43-character verifier, keeps it, and adds `code_challenge` and `code_challenge_method=S256` to the URL; `exchange_code/2` sends the `code_verifier`. Call `build_authorization_url/2` before `exchange_code/2` with the same authenticator.

#### Token caching

The token manager caches the access token in memory and refreshes it before its declared expiry, by one margin of at most `60` seconds and never more than half of `expires_in` (a 60 second token is reused for 30 seconds). Expiry is measured on the monotonic clock, so a wall clock change does not extend or cut a token's life. A token response without `expires_in` is cached until the API answers 401 or the token is invalidated.

#### Client authentication method

OAuth2 clients can transmit their `client_id` and `client_secret` to the token endpoint two ways (RFC 6749 §2.3.1):

- `:body` (default) sends them as `application/x-www-form-urlencoded` parameters in the request body.
- `:basic` sends them as an HTTP Basic `Authorization` header.

Override the default if your authorization server only accepts one form:

```elixir file
defmodule BasicClientAuthentication do
  def authenticator do
    {{moduleName}}.Auth.OAuth.OAuth2ClientCredentialsAuthenticator.new(
      "https://api.example.com", "client-id", "client-secret", "https://auth.example.com/token", [],
      client_auth_method: :basic)
  end
end
```

{{/hasAnyOAuth2}}
{{#hasServers}}
## Servers

If the OpenAPI spec defines multiple servers, the generated `{{moduleName}}.Servers` module exposes each as a `server_N/0` function returning a `ServerConfiguration`, plus a `{{moduleName}}.Servers.all/0` list. A server the spec gives a name is also found by that name with `Servers.by_name("name")`, which fails with `ArgumentError` when no server has the name. Pass the desired server's URL to the client:

```elixir file
defmodule ServerClient do
  def client do
    url = {{moduleName}}.Servers.server_0() |> {{moduleName}}.ServerConfiguration.url()
    {{moduleName}}.{{clientClassName}}.with_token(url, "your-token")
  end
end
```

{{/hasServers}}

## Testing

The `Authenticator` behaviour is the seam for tests: substitute a fake authenticator that returns a known header map, and assert your code calls the API the way you expect.

```elixir file
defmodule FakeAuthenticator do
  @behaviour {{moduleName}}.Auth.Authenticator

  defstruct []

  @impl true
  def host(_), do: "https://api.example.com"

  @impl true
  def auth_headers(_), do: %{"Authorization" => "Bearer test-token"}

  @impl true
  def query_params(_), do: %{}

  @impl true
  def cookie_params(_), do: %{}
end

defmodule FakeClient do
  def client, do: {{moduleName}}.{{clientClassName}}.new(%FakeAuthenticator{})
end
```

## Error Handling

Every failure the SDK raises or returns is one of the exceptions listed by `{{moduleName}}.Errors.{{errorPrefix}}Error.exceptions/0`, and `{{moduleName}}.Errors.{{errorPrefix}}Error.{{errorPrefixSnake}}_error?/1` is true for each of them: that is the root to catch last. Every error for an HTTP response or a network failure derives from `ApiError`; encoding and decoding failures are `SerializationError`, and the token flows add `OAuth2ServerError` (the token endpoint answered 4xx or 5xx) and `OAuth2TokenError` (it answered unusably). The error hierarchy is:

- `{{errorPrefix}}Error` (root grouping: `exceptions/0` and `{{errorPrefixSnake}}_error?/1`)
  - `SerializationError` (a body could not be encoded or decoded)
  - `OAuth2ServerError` (token endpoint answered 4xx or 5xx)
  - `OAuth2TokenError` (token endpoint answered unusably)
  - `ApiError` (an HTTP response or a network failure)
    - `ClientError` (4xx)
      - `BadRequestError` (400)
      - `UnauthorizedError` (401)
      - `ForbiddenError` (403)
      - `NotFoundError` (404)
      - `ConflictError` (409)
      - `UnprocessableEntityError` (422)
      - `TooManyRequestsError` (429)
      - `PaymentRequiredError` (402)
      - `MethodNotAllowedError` (405)
      - `RequestTimeoutError` (408)
      - `GoneError` (410)
      - `PayloadTooLargeError` (413)
      - `UnsupportedMediaTypeError` (415)
      - `LockedError` (423)
    - `ServerError` (5xx)
      - `InternalServerError` (500)
      - `NotImplementedError` (501)
      - `BadGatewayError` (502)
      - `ServiceUnavailableError` (503)
      - `GatewayTimeoutError` (504)
    - `NetworkError` (no HTTP response, status 0)
      - `NetworkTimeoutError` (the request timed out, status 0)

{{#apiInfo}}{{#apis}}{{#-first}}{{#operations}}{{#operation}}{{#-first}}```elixir file
defmodule ErrorHandling do
  def {{operationId}}_or_report(client{{#vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgsNoServer}}, {{#isPath}}{{paramName}}{{/isPath}}{{#isBody}}{{paramName}}{{/isBody}}{{#isOptions}}{{#vendorExtensions.op.optionsParamRequired}}options{{/vendorExtensions.op.optionsParamRequired}}{{^vendorExtensions.op.optionsParamRequired}}options \\ nil{{/vendorExtensions.op.optionsParamRequired}}{{/isOptions}}{{/vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgsNoServer}}) do
    case {{moduleName}}.Api.{{classname}}.{{operationId}}(client.{{clientPropertyName}}{{#vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgsNoServer}}, {{paramName}}{{/vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgsNoServer}}) do
      {:ok, result} ->
        IO.inspect(result)

      {:error, %{{moduleName}}.Errors.NotFoundError{} = e} ->
        IO.puts("Not found: #{e.message}")

      # Elixir has no inheritance: each error in the hierarchy above has a
      # predicate that is true for it and for every error below it.
      {:error, e} ->
        cond do
          {{moduleName}}.Errors.ClientError.client_error?(e) ->
            IO.puts("Client error #{e.status_code}: #{e.message}")

          {{moduleName}}.Errors.ServerError.server_error?(e) ->
            IO.puts("Server error: #{e.message}")

          {{moduleName}}.Errors.{{errorPrefix}}Error.{{errorPrefixSnake}}_error?(e) ->
            IO.puts("SDK error: #{Exception.message(e)}")

          true ->
            IO.puts("Error: #{Exception.message(e)}")
        end
    end
  end
end
```{{/-first}}{{/operation}}{{/operations}}{{/-first}}{{/apis}}{{/apiInfo}}

An error that answers a request carries `request_method` (`"GET"`) and `request_path` (the path without the query string); its message has a `Request: GET /path` line after the status line.

The text of an `ApiError` (`Exception.message/1`) cuts the response body at 1 KiB with a `... (N more bytes)` suffix, writes control characters as `\xNN`, and masks credential response headers (`Authorization`, `Cookie`, `Set-Cookie`, `WWW-Authenticate` and any name containing key, token or secret). The full body stays on `response_body` and the full headers on `response_headers`.

## Configuration

### Custom Transport Options

```elixir file
defmodule ProxiedClient do
  def client do
    authenticator = {{moduleName}}.Auth.BearerAuthenticator.new("https://api.example.com", "your-token")

    transport =
      {{moduleName}}.TransportOptions.new(
        proxy: "http://proxy:3128",
        timeout: 5000
      )

    {{moduleName}}.{{clientClassName}}.new(authenticator, transport)
  end
end
```

### Retries

`max_retries` on `TransportOptions` is how many times a failed call is tried again (default `2`; `0` turns retries off). A call is retried after a network error that happened before any response, and after a response with status 408, 409, 429 or 500 to 599 other than 501 (a timeout, 400, 401, 403, 404, 422 and 501 are not retried). The wait starts at 0.5 s and doubles up to 8 s, less up to 25% jitter; a `Retry-After` header (seconds or an HTTP date) replaces it, capped at 60 s. Every retry is a fresh request: headers, authentication included, are rebuilt. Retries share the single end-to-end `timeout` and never extend it.

```elixir file
defmodule NoRetriesClient do
  def client do
    authenticator = {{moduleName}}.Auth.BearerAuthenticator.new("https://api.example.com", "your-token")
    transport = {{moduleName}}.TransportOptions.new(max_retries: 0)
    {{moduleName}}.{{clientClassName}}.new(authenticator, transport)
  end
end
```

`TransportOptions.new(max_response_bytes: n)` bounds a buffered response body to `n` bytes (off by default): a body that passes it, on the wire or once decompressed, fails with `ResponseTooLargeError` and is never retried. A streamed response is not bounded by it.

### Streaming

An operation whose success response is binary (an octet stream, an image, `type: string, format: binary`) returns a `ByteStream` instead of the whole body in memory, so a large download never has to fit in memory. The call returns as soon as the response headers arrive, and the body is read as you consume it. A `ByteStream` has the status code, the lower-cased headers and an idempotent `close/1` that aborts the connection instead of draining it, so close it when you stop early (in an `after` block). `read_all/1` reads the rest into memory and leaves the stream closed.
{{#hasBinaryStreamOperation}}

```elixir file
defmodule Download do
{{#binaryStreamOperation}}
  def save_to(client{{#vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgsNoServer}}, {{paramName}}{{/vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgsNoServer}}, path) do
    {:ok, stream} = {{moduleName}}.Api.{{#binaryStreamGroup}}{{classname}}{{/binaryStreamGroup}}.{{operationId}}(client.{{#binaryStreamGroup}}{{clientPropertyName}}{{/binaryStreamGroup}}{{#vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgsNoServer}}, {{paramName}}{{/vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgsNoServer}})

    try do
      IO.puts(stream.status_code)
      Enum.into(stream, File.stream!(path))
    after
      {{moduleName}}.ByteStream.close(stream)
    end
  end
{{/binaryStreamOperation}}
end
```
{{/hasBinaryStreamOperation}}

The call `timeout` covers the wait for the response headers and stops there. `idle_timeout` (in `TransportOptions`, default 60 seconds, `0` or `nil` disables it) bounds the gap between two reads of the body and raises `NetworkTimeoutError`. Retries, redirects, token refresh and the error contract apply up to the headers exactly as for a buffered call; a request is never retried after the first body byte, and a non-2xx status raises the usual typed error with its body read up to 64 KiB and never returns a stream. A connection reset, or a body that ends before its declared `Content-Length`, raises `StreamError`, a `NetworkError` with status 0. `{{clientClassName}}.close/1` cancels the streams that are still open, and their next read raises the closed-client error. A custom transport that does not implement `send_streaming` fails a stream call with `RuntimeError` naming it, and its buffered calls keep working.

An operation whose request body is raw binary (`type: string, format: binary`) takes a `BodySource` instead of buffering the bytes, so a large upload never has to fit in memory. A `BodySource` is replayable, made with `BodySource.of(bytes)`, `BodySource.of_file(path)` or `BodySource.from_factory(factory)` (a function that opens a fresh stream each time), or single use, made with `BodySource.once(stream)`. The body is sent as it is read: a known length (bytes, a file, or a length you pass) goes out as `Content-Length` and an unknown length is sent chunked. A replayable body is read again for a retry after a 5xx, a 429 or a network error and for a 307 or 308 redirect. A single-use body is never retried, so the original error is returned unchanged, and a 307 or 308 is refused with `Refusing to replay a streamed request body`; it is never buffered to make a replay possible. The SDK never closes a stream you pass to `once` and closes the streams it opens itself from a file or a factory. Multipart bodies stay buffered. A 401 that refreshes the token follows the same rule, and an event stream request with a single-use body is never reconnected.

An operation whose success response is JSON Lines (`application/x-ndjson`, `application/jsonl`, `application/jsonlines`) or server-sent events (`text/event-stream`) has no buffered function. It has a `<operation>_stream` function that takes the operation's parameters, its options struct and the trailing `opts` keyword list that carries the stream settings, and returns `{:ok, stream}`, a `JsonLinesStream` of the schema type or an `SseStream` of `ServerSentEvent` (`id`, `event`, `data`, `retry` and the raw `data` text; the `data` is the schema of the `data`, or a string). Enumerate it, and close it when you stop early. A JSON Lines line that does not decode, and a line or event longer than `max_event_bytes` (1 MiB), raise `SerializationError` and end the stream.
{{#hasJsonLinesStreamOperation}}

```elixir file
defmodule ExportLines do
{{#jsonLinesStreamOperation}}
  def run(client{{#vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgsNoServer}}, {{paramName}}{{/vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgsNoServer}}) do
    {:ok, stream} = {{moduleName}}.Api.{{#jsonLinesStreamGroup}}{{classname}}{{/jsonLinesStreamGroup}}.{{operationId}}_stream(client.{{#jsonLinesStreamGroup}}{{clientPropertyName}}{{/jsonLinesStreamGroup}}{{#vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgsNoServer}}, {{paramName}}{{/vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgsNoServer}})

    try do
      Enum.each(stream, fn item -> IO.inspect(item) end)
    after
      {{moduleName}}.JsonLinesStream.close(stream)
    end
  end
{{/jsonLinesStreamOperation}}
end
```
{{/hasJsonLinesStreamOperation}}
{{#hasSseStreamOperation}}

```elixir file
defmodule FollowEvents do
{{#sseStreamOperation}}
  def run(client{{#vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgsNoServer}}, {{paramName}}{{/vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgsNoServer}}) do
    {:ok, stream} = {{moduleName}}.Api.{{#sseStreamGroup}}{{classname}}{{/sseStreamGroup}}.{{operationId}}_stream(client.{{#sseStreamGroup}}{{clientPropertyName}}{{/sseStreamGroup}}{{#vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgsNoServer}}, {{paramName}}{{/vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgsNoServer}}, max_reconnects: 3)

    try do
      Enum.each(stream, fn item -> IO.inspect(item) end)
    after
      {{moduleName}}.SseStream.close(stream)
    end
  end
{{/sseStreamOperation}}
end
```
{{/hasSseStreamOperation}}

An event stream reconnects when the connection ends, fails or stays silent for the idle timeout: it asks again with fresh headers and `Last-Event-ID`, waits the server's `retry` value or `reconnect_delay` (1 second, doubling to 30 seconds with jitter), and raises the last failure after `max_reconnects` failures in a row without an event (5; 0 for a request with a body; `-1` is unlimited). It never reconnects after a 204, a status other than 408, 429 and 5xx, a response that is not `text/event-stream`, a cancellation or `{{clientClassName}}.close/1`. An event named `error` raises `ApiError` with the response status and the data as its body, and a comment line such as a heartbeat counts as activity for the idle timeout. The generator option `sseTerminator` names a data value (such as `[DONE]`) that ends the stream and is not delivered. `opts` also takes `idle_timeout` and `last_event_id`.

### Pagination

An operation that returns a list one page at a time has a `<operation>_pages` function next to its plain function. It takes the same parameters in the same order and returns a `Paginator`, an `Enumerable` of the items. The function sends no request: the first request goes out when you first read an item or a page, and each next request goes out only when you ask for more, so stopping early sends nothing further and leaves nothing to close. Enumerate the paginator for the items, or call `Paginator.pages/1` for `Page` structs with `items`, `next_cursor` and `result`, the same `ApiResult` that the `_with_http_info` variant returns, so the status and headers stay reachable. Enumerating again starts again from the starting position.
{{#hasPagedOperation}}

```elixir file
defmodule ListEverything do
{{#pagedOperation}}
  def read(client{{#vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgsNoServer}}, {{paramName}}{{/vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgsNoServer}}) do
    list = {{moduleName}}.Api.{{#pagedGroup}}{{classname}}{{/pagedGroup}}.{{vendorExtensions.op.pagesMethodName}}(client.{{#pagedGroup}}{{clientPropertyName}}{{/pagedGroup}}{{#vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgsNoServer}}, {{paramName}}{{/vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgsNoServer}})

    Enum.each(list, fn item -> IO.inspect(item) end)

    for page <- {{moduleName}}.Paginator.pages(list) do
      IO.puts("#{length(page.items)} items, status #{page.result.status_code}")
    end
  end
{{/pagedOperation}}
end
```
{{/hasPagedOperation}}

The cursor you pass to the function is the starting position, and the next cursor of a page resumes the list: pass it as the starting cursor of a new call. The page size and every other parameter, option and header are repeated unchanged on each page request, and each page is an ordinary call with its own retries, timeout, redirects, authentication and redaction. An error on a later page is raised from the read that needed it, after the items of the earlier pages were delivered, and ends the enumeration; the SDK never skips a page. A list ends when the next cursor is absent, `nil` or empty, and an empty page with a next cursor continues. A page that returns the cursor that was just sent raises `PaginationError`, a member of the `{{errorPrefix}}Error` family, after its items were delivered, instead of looping. There is no total limit and no collect-all: use `Enum.take/2`.

How each style ends and resumes: an id list passes the `last_id` or `next_cursor` of each page as the starting cursor of the next request and ends when a declared `has_more` is false. An offset list starts at the offset you pass, sends the offset past the items it received on each next request, and ends on an empty page or when a declared `has_more` is false. A page-number list sends the page you pass, or none, and then asks for the page after each one, and ends on an empty page or when a declared `has_more` is false. A next URL list takes the URL in the body of each page, follows it only within the origin of the client, and ends when the property is absent, `null` or empty. A cursor, offset or page that does not advance raises `PaginationError` as a token cursor does.

A list that is followed by a `Link` header (`rel="next"`, RFC 8288) takes the next URL from the header of each response. A next URL that leaves the scheme, host or port of the client is refused with `PaginationError` and nothing, no credentials either, is sent to it; such a function has no starting cursor, so it cannot resume.

A list whose page body carries the next URL in a property (a next URL list) is followed in the same way as a `Link` list: the URL is taken from the body of each response, a next URL that leaves the scheme, host or port of the client is refused with `PaginationError`, nothing is sent to it, and such a method cannot resume from a saved position.

Pagination covers the token cursor, id cursor, offset, page number, `Link` header and next URL in the body styles. The override option and the vendor hints are as described in the readme.

The generator finds a paged operation by its names and shape. The operation must be a GET with one JSON 2xx response, and it must match exactly one of these styles. A token cursor is one query parameter among `cursor`, `after`, `page_token`, `next_token`, `continuation_token` and `marker`, paired with a next-cursor property among `next_cursor`, `next_page_token`, `next_token`, `next_marker`, `next_continuation_token`, `continuation_token` and `next` of the same type. An id cursor is one query parameter among `starting_after`, `after_id` and `after`, paired with a boolean `has_more` and a string `last_id` or `next_cursor` property. A `Link` list has a `Link` header declared on the response. An offset list has an `offset` query parameter with a `limit`, and a page-number list has a `page` query parameter with one of `per_page`, `page_size`, `size` or `limit`. A next URL list has no cursor parameter and a string property with `format: uri` or `uri-reference`, or one named `next_url`, `next_uri`, `nextlink` or `next_link`, that is optional or nullable. An operation that matches more than one style is ambiguous, and one that is streamed is never paged; either keeps only its plain method, and the generation log names it and says why. The `x-fern-pagination` and `x-speakeasy-pagination` (cursor type) extensions are read as hints. The generator option `paginationOverrides` (a JSON object that maps an operation id to `none` or to `{style, requestParam, responseProperty, itemsProperty, hasMoreProperty}`, where the style is `token`, `link`, `id`, `offset`, `page` or `body`) wins over a hint and over inference, and `pagination=off` switches the feature off.

### Custom HTTP client

Pass Req options such as an `:adapter` function or a `:plug` through `req_options`. The SDK's own settings (timeouts, redirect policy, proxy, TLS, user agent) are applied on top, and the SDK never closes anything you supply. Combining `:connect_options` or `:finch` with `proxy`, `ca_cert_path` or `verify_ssl: false` raises `ArgumentError`.

```elixir file
defmodule AdapterClient do
  def client do
    authenticator = {{moduleName}}.Auth.BearerAuthenticator.new("https://api.example.com", "your-token")
    adapter = fn request -> {request, Req.Response.new(status: 200)} end
    transport = {{moduleName}}.TransportOptions.new(req_options: [adapter: adapter])
    {{moduleName}}.{{clientClassName}}.new(authenticator, transport)
  end
end
```

### Per-call options

Every operation takes an optional options struct, even one with no query, header, form or cookie parameter. It carries `timeout` (milliseconds), `headers` (a map) and `max_retries`; there is no per-call credential. The same three settings can be passed as `:timeout`, `:headers` and `:max_retries` in the trailing `opts` keyword list, which wins over the struct. Each replaces the client's own setting for that one call.

{{#hasExampleOperation}}
{{#exampleOperation}}
```elixir file
defmodule PerCall do
  def call(client) do
    options = %{{moduleName}}.Api.Options.{{vendorExtensions.op.optionsClassName}}{timeout: 5_000, headers: %{"X-Trace" => "1"}, max_retries: 0}
    {{moduleName}}.Api.{{#exampleOperationGroup}}{{classname}}{{/exampleOperationGroup}}.{{operationId}}(client.{{#exampleOperationGroup}}{{clientPropertyName}}{{/exampleOperationGroup}}, options)
    {{moduleName}}.Api.{{#exampleOperationGroup}}{{classname}}{{/exampleOperationGroup}}.{{operationId}}(client.{{#exampleOperationGroup}}{{clientPropertyName}}{{/exampleOperationGroup}}, nil, timeout: 5_000)
  end
end
```
{{/exampleOperation}}
{{/hasExampleOperation}}

### Credentials

{{#hasClientSchemes}}
The client takes one optional credential for each security scheme, named after the scheme, in the keyword list that is its third argument: {{#clientSchemes}}`{{#lambda.elixirSnake}}{{paramName}}{{/lambda.elixirSnake}}`{{^-last}}, {{/-last}}{{/clientSchemes}}. An operation uses the credential of the first scheme its own security requirement lists that the client holds; the authenticator in the first argument serves an operation that none of them matches, and an operation declared `security: []` sends no credential. There is no per-call override, and the client is refused when it gets no credential at all.

```elixir file
defmodule SchemeCredential do
  def client(credential) do
    {{moduleName}}.{{clientClassName}}.new(nil, nil, {{#clientSchemes}}{{#-first}}{{#lambda.elixirSnake}}{{paramName}}{{/lambda.elixirSnake}}: credential{{/-first}}{{/clientSchemes}})
  end
end
```

{{/hasClientSchemes}}
Pass `nil` for a credential (or leave it out of a security scheme's authenticator) and the authenticator reads it once, when it is built, from an environment variable. A value you pass wins, an empty variable counts as unset, and a missing required credential raises `ArgumentError` when the client is built. A bearer token or an API key may be a zero-argument function that returns it, called for every request.
{{#hasSecuritySchemeEnvVars}}

Each security scheme reads its own variables, named `{{envVarPrefix}}_<FIELD>`, or `{{envVarPrefix}}_<SCHEME>_<FIELD>` for every scheme that shares a field name with another:

| Security scheme | Credential | Environment variable |
|---|---|---|
{{#securitySchemeEnvVars}}
| `{{schemeId}}` | `{{#lambda.elixirSnake}}{{field}}{{/lambda.elixirSnake}}` | `{{envName}}` |
{{/securitySchemeEnvVars}}

An authenticator you build without a scheme (`BearerAuthenticator`, `BasicAuthenticator`, `ApiKeyAuthenticator`) reads `{{envVarPrefix}}_TOKEN`, `{{envVarPrefix}}_USERNAME`, `{{envVarPrefix}}_PASSWORD` or `{{envVarPrefix}}_API_KEY`. Set `envVarPrefix` when generating to change the `{{envVarPrefix}}` prefix.
{{/hasSecuritySchemeEnvVars}}

```elixir file
defmodule RotatingToken do
  def authenticator do
    {{moduleName}}.Auth.BearerAuthenticator.new("https://api.example.com", fn -> System.fetch_env!("{{envVarPrefix}}_TOKEN") end)
  end
end
```

### Cancellation

There is no cancellation argument. The SDK uses the platform's own mechanism: stop the process that is making the call, or set `timeout` on `TransportOptions`, which ends the call with `NetworkTimeoutError`.

### Hooks and telemetry

Every operation calls one small, optional hook around the call. The hook can edit the request headers before the request is sent and observe the status or the error afterwards, and without a hook set the call does nothing extra.

A hook is a module that implements the `{{moduleName}}.CallHook` behaviour, or `{module, opts}`. `start(info, headers)` returns the headers to send and a state, or `nil`. `info` is a `CallInfo` (`operation_id`, `method`, `url_template`, `url`, `server_address`), `headers` is the final request header map and `outcome` is a `CallOutcome` (`status_code`, `headers`, `error`). `start` runs once per call, `finish(state, outcome)` exactly once on every way the call can end, an error raised by `start` reaches the caller and an error raised by `finish` is swallowed. `CallHook.chain([first, second])` runs several hooks in order.

```elixir file
defmodule TenantHook do
  @behaviour {{moduleName}}.CallHook

  @impl true
  def start(_info, headers), do: {Map.put(headers, "X-Tenant", "a"), nil}

  @impl true
  def finish(_state, _outcome), do: :ok
end

defmodule TenantClient do
  def build do
    {{moduleName}}.{{clientClassName}}.with_token("https://api.example.com", "token", nil, call_hook: TenantHook)
  end
end
```

For OpenTelemetry the SDK ships no adapter, and the README shows a `TelemetryCallHook` recipe that records one span, one request count and one duration per call.

### Closing the client

`{{moduleName}}.{{clientClassName}}.close/1` closes the client and is idempotent. A call already in flight finishes, and a call that starts after it raises `RuntimeError` ("ApiClient has been closed and can no longer send requests"). Streams that are still open are cancelled. The processes the SDK started (OAuth2 token managers, OpenID Connect discovery) are stopped when the last call in flight is over, or at once when none is. A transport you supply through `req_options` is never closed by the SDK.

```elixir file
defmodule Closing do
  def close(client), do: :ok = {{moduleName}}.{{clientClassName}}.close(client)
end
```

### Cookie parameters

A cookie parameter is sent raw when every character is a legal RFC 6265 cookie-octet (printable ASCII except space, double quote, comma, semicolon and backslash). Any other character raises `ArgumentError` before the request is sent: percent-encode the value yourself first.

### Raw responses, timeouts and the request id

Each operation has a `_with_http_info` variant returning an `ApiResult` (`status_code`, `data`, `raw_body`, `headers`). `timeout` on `TransportOptions` is one end-to-end budget in positive milliseconds; a call that exceeds it ends with `NetworkTimeoutError`. With `inject_request_id: true` an `X-Request-ID` header is added to each request unless you set one.

## API Methods

Each API group is exposed as a typed field on the client (e.g., `client.{{#apiInfo}}{{#apis}}{{#-first}}{{#operations}}{{clientPropertyName}}{{/operations}}{{/-first}}{{/apis}}{{/apiInfo}}`). API classes have methods that correspond to OpenAPI operations, accepting typed request parameters and returning typed response models.

## Models

Models are generated as Elixir structs under the `{{moduleName}}.Models` namespace. A property the spec marks required (and that has no default) is an enforced key: the struct cannot be built without it.

{{#models}}{{#model}}{{#-first}}
```elixir file
defmodule ModelCopy do
  def copy_of(%{{moduleName}}.Models.{{classname}}{} = model) do
{{#vars}}
{{#-first}}
    %{model | {{name}}: model.{{name}}}
{{/-first}}
{{/vars}}
  end
end
```
{{/-first}}{{/model}}{{/models}}

Models never change in place. Make a changed copy with the struct update syntax: `%{model | name: "new"}`.

## Binary / File Uploads

A raw binary request body is a `BodySource` (see Streaming). Multipart file parameters are typed as `binary()`. A binary response body is returned as a `ByteStream`, an `Enumerable` of binaries (see Streaming).

## Comment Style

Never place a comment on the same line as code. Use `#` comments; `@moduledoc` and `@doc` are fine.

```good
# This explains the logic
x = 1
```

```bad
x = 1  # This explains the logic
```
